  A1_10="""Row 1: IDLE, 10 SMALL-STEP POSES forming ONE single slow loop (an animation tool will create extra in-between frames, so each pose differs from the previous one by only a SMALL step, a few pixels of movement, never a big jump; the cycle closes back to pose 1). The 10 cells are read in order: row 1 left to right = poses 1 to 5, then row 2 left to right = poses 6 to 10. ALL 10 cells are filled. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally, his feet rest near the bottom of the cell (about 8% margin) and his whole body, from the top of his hair to his boots, fills about 80% of the cell height; same scale, same foot position, same horizontal center in all 10 cells; no wasted empty space around him. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose, only their position and tilt follow the head; the scarf tail and the sash tails may sway a little. In EVERY pose: the katana stays sheathed on his back, always whole, the hilt over his right shoulder, and his right hand reaches up behind the shoulder and clearly grips the hilt; the left hand hangs loose and relaxed; both feet stay planted in the same spot. Pose 1: neutral confident stance, weight on the right leg, shoulders level. Pose 2: weight moves slightly toward the center, the left shoulder starts to dip. Pose 3: weight on the left leg, left shoulder lower, head tilts slightly. Pose 4: weight fully on the left leg, hips sway left (the left peak of the sway). Pose 5: weight returns toward the center, shoulders level again. Pose 6: both shoulders begin to roll up and back (small). Pose 7: shoulders up and back, chest slightly open, chin slightly raised (a moderate roll, the peak). Pose 8: the shoulders drop and roll forward. Pose 9: weight shifts back to the right leg, head tilts slightly the other way. Pose 10: almost identical to pose 1 (a tiny variation) so the loop closes smoothly. Every step between consecutive poses is small and smooth; together they read as a slow, visible movement. No motion blur, no big effects.""",
  A1_20="""Row 1: IDLE, 20 SMALL-STEP POSES forming ONE long, lively, non-repetitive loop made of several different actions in sequence (an animation tool will create extra in-between frames, so each pose differs from the previous one by only a SMALL step, a few pixels of movement, never a big jump; but the whole sequence travels through a LARGE, expressive range of motion, clearly bigger than a simple breathing idle; the cycle closes back to pose 1). The 20 cells are read in order: row 1 left to right = poses 1 to 5, row 2 = poses 6 to 10, row 3 = poses 11 to 15, row 4 = poses 16 to 20. ALL 20 cells are filled. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally, his feet rest near the bottom of the cell (about 8% margin) and his whole body, from the top of his hair to his boots, fills about 80% of the cell height; same scale, same foot position, same horizontal center in all 20 cells; both feet stay planted in the same spot; no wasted empty space. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose, only their position and tilt follow the head; the scarf tail and the sash tails sway freely. In EVERY pose: the katana stays sheathed on his back, always whole, the hilt over his right shoulder, and his right hand reaches up behind the shoulder and clearly grips the hilt. ACTIONS IN ORDER: (1) poses 1 to 5, a wide weight shift: neutral stance, then the hips sway far to the left with the left shoulder dipping, then back to center; (2) poses 6 to 10, a big shoulder roll: both shoulders lift up and back, the chest opens, the chin rises, then they drop and roll forward; (3) poses 11 to 14, a head and neck movement: the head tilts to the left, comes back, tilts to the right, comes back, while the scarf tail swings with it; (4) poses 15 to 18, the left hand stretches: the free left arm swings out a little, the fingers open and close slowly, the arm returns, and the chest takes a deep breath (rises, then falls); (5) poses 19 and 20, a relaxed settle: the weight returns to the right leg, shoulders level, and pose 20 is almost identical to pose 1 so the loop closes smoothly. Every step between consecutive poses is small and smooth; together they read as a slow, lively, visible movement. No motion blur, no big effects.""",
  A1_25="""Row 1: IDLE, 25 TINY-STEP POSES forming ONE slow, smooth, continuous loop: a fighting ready-stance in which the hand on the katana sways left and right like a pendulum. Each pose differs from the previous one by only a TINY step (one or two pixels of movement), never a jump; the cycle closes back to pose 1. The 25 cells are read in order: each row left to right, from the top row to the bottom row (row 1 = poses 1 to 5, row 2 = poses 6 to 10, row 3 = poses 11 to 15, row 4 = poses 16 to 20, row 5 = poses 21 to 25). ALL 25 cells are filled. STANCE (the same in every pose): a wide fighting stance, the legs apart (feet about twice the shoulder width), knees slightly bent, the body a little lowered, both feet planted in the same spot, weight even on both legs. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally, his feet rest near the bottom of the cell (about 8% margin) and his whole body, from the top of his hair to his boots, fills about 80% of the cell height; same scale, same foot position, same horizontal center in all 25 cells; no wasted empty space. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose, only their position and tilt follow the head; the scarf tail and the sash tails sway softly with the movement. THE KATANA: it stays sheathed on his back, always whole, the hilt over his right shoulder; his right hand always clearly grips the hilt and moves it side to side: pose 1 the hand is at the center, poses 2 to 7 it drifts slowly to the LEFT (pose 7 is the leftmost), poses 8 to 13 it comes back to the center (pose 13 is almost identical to pose 1), poses 14 to 19 it drifts slowly to the RIGHT (pose 19 is the rightmost), poses 20 to 25 it returns toward the center (pose 25 is a tiny step before pose 1). The shoulders and torso lean very slightly in the same direction as the hand. THE FREE HAND: the left hand (without the katana) stays still in a relaxed guard position near his hip, always in the same place relative to his body, simply following the body's small lean; it does not move on its own and never changes shape. Smooth, slow, tiny steps; no motion blur, no big effects.""",
  walk_25="""Row 1: WALK, 25 TINY-STEP POSES, calm and confident, with the right hand on the katana hilt. NATURALNESS (mandatory rule): movement never appears out of nowhere, it HAPPENS. Poses 1 to 5 are the START of the walk: pose 1 is EXACTLY the idle fighting stance (wide legs, knees bent, both fists in guard at waist height, the right hand at the waist); pose by pose the RIGHT hand rises from the waist, travels up behind the right shoulder and grips the katana hilt (pose 5: the hand firmly on the hilt), while the legs slowly come closer together and straighten into a relaxed upright stance and the weight settles onto the left foot, ready to step. Poses 6 to 25 are ONE complete looping walk cycle of TWO steps (20 poses; pose 25 flows seamlessly into pose 6): a calm, confident, natural walk to the right, IN PLACE (treadmill style: his body stays at the same horizontal position in every cell and the feet cycle underneath him). Natural walking mechanics: the heel touches the ground first and the foot rolls forward; the hips shift the weight smoothly; the torso stays upright with a tiny counter-rotation; the head bobs very slightly; the right hand STAYS on the hilt in all poses from 5 to 25 (the right shoulder and elbow follow the body, the hand never lets go); the LEFT arm swings naturally forward and backward opposite to the leading leg, its hand relaxed; the scarf tail and the sash tail trail behind and sway with a slight delay (secondary motion). Each pose differs from the previous one by only a TINY, smooth step, never a jump; contact poses and passing poses are all clearly readable. The 25 cells are read in order: each row left to right, from the top row to the bottom row. ALL 25 cells are filled. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally, his feet touch the ground line near the bottom of the cell (about 8% margin) and his whole body, from the top of his hair to his boots, fills about 80% of the cell height; same scale and same ground line in all 25 cells; no wasted empty space. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose; the katana stays sheathed on his back, always whole, the hilt over his right shoulder. No motion blur, no big effects.""",
  hop_3="""Row 1: HOP, 3 poses, one pose per cell, read left to right: the SUBTLE quick jump forward used to reach an enemy (a short hop, NOT a big leap). Pose 1, CROUCH (anticipation): knees bent, body lowered and leaning slightly forward, weight loaded on the legs, the right hand on the katana hilt behind the shoulder, the left hand low at his side. Pose 2, AIR: a subtle hop, both feet lifted only about 10% of the cell height above the ground line, the body stretched slightly forward, the legs trailing a little behind, the left arm slightly back, the scarf tail and the sash tail trailing backward. Pose 3, LANDING: both feet back on the ground line, knees bent absorbing the impact, the torso leaning forward ready to strike, the left hand forward in guard, the right hand still on the hilt. The three poses are clearly different from each other, but the whole motion is subtle and quick. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally; the ground line is at the SAME height in all 3 cells (about 8% from the bottom of the cell); in pose 2 only the feet are above that line (the body is raised by the same amount); a standing Kaen would fill about 75% of the cell height; same scale in all 3 cells. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose; the katana stays sheathed on his back, always whole, the hilt over his right shoulder. No motion blur, no dust, no big effects.""",
  entry_6="""Row 1: ENTRY, 6 poses, read in order (row 1 left to right = poses 1 to 3, row 2 left to right = poses 4 to 6): Kaen drops in from the air, lands with one hand on the ground and pulls himself together. NATURALNESS (mandatory rule): movement never appears out of nowhere, it HAPPENS; every pose grows out of the previous one, and the last pose is EXACTLY the first pose of his idle. Pose 1, FALLING FAST: high above the ground, falling feet-first, knees slightly bent, arms spread a little for balance, the scarf tail and the sash tail streaming UPWARD because of the fall, the katana sheathed on his back. Pose 2, ABOUT TO LAND: lower, the legs extending down toward the ground, the body leaning slightly forward, the left hand reaching down, the scarf and sash still streaming upward. Pose 3, IMPACT: the landing, deep crouch with the knees bent hard, the LEFT hand planted flat on the ground, the right arm pulled back (the right hand near the katana hilt), the head low, the scarf and sash whipping forward and down from the impact. Pose 4, HOLD: still crouched, the left hand still planted on the ground, the head starting to lift, the scarf and sash settling. Pose 5, RISING: the left hand leaves the ground, the body straightens halfway, the head up, looking forward. Pose 6, RECOMPOSED: standing in his wide fighting stance, legs apart with the knees bent, both fists in guard at waist height, calm confident face, the scarf and sash settled (this pose must be identical to the first pose of his fighting idle). FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally; the ground line is at the SAME height in all 6 cells (about 8% from the bottom of the cell); a standing Kaen fills only about 55% of the cell height, leaving room above him; in pose 1 his feet are about 30% of the cell height above the ground line, in pose 2 about 12% above it, in poses 3 to 6 his feet are on the ground line; same scale in all 6 cells. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose; the katana stays sheathed on his back, always whole, the hilt over his right shoulder. No dust, no impact marks, no motion blur, no big effects.""",
  strong_20="""Row 1: STRONG ATTACK, 20 poses, read in order (each row left to right: 5 poses per row, row 1 = poses 1 to 5, row 2 = poses 6 to 10, row 3 = poses 11 to 15, row 4 = poses 16 to 20): Kaen charges the katana with fire, makes ONE huge downward cut and sheathes it. NATURALNESS (mandatory rule): movement never appears out of nowhere, it HAPPENS; every pose grows out of the previous one. Pose 1 is EXACTLY the landing pose of his hop (knees bent, torso leaning forward, left hand forward in guard, the right hand on the katana hilt behind his shoulder). DRAW, poses 1 to 3: the right hand pulls the katana out of the sheath over his shoulder; pose 2 the blade is half out, pose 3 the blade is fully drawn (the black sheath stays empty on his back). CHARGE, poses 4 to 9: pose 4 he lowers into a wide stance and holds the blade with both hands beside his body; pose 5 small solid red and orange embers appear along the blade; pose 6 the embers grow into a solid flame hugging the blade; pose 7 he raises the blade slowly overhead, flame growing; pose 8 the blade is fully raised high above his head with both hands, the flame at its biggest, the body leaning back (anticipation, all the weight loaded); pose 9 a short pause at the top, the body tensed, the knees bending more. THE CUT, poses 10 to 14: pose 10 the body lunges forward and the blade starts down; pose 11 the blade is halfway down, the arms driving; pose 12 the blade strikes down in front of him with the arms fully extended and the front knee bent deep (the strongest pose of the whole attack, solid bright flame along the blade); pose 13 the follow-through, the blade low near the ground, flame fading to embers; pose 14 he holds the pose, the embers dying out. RECOVER, poses 15 to 17: pose 15 he straightens slowly and lifts the blade to one hand at his side, the flame gone; pose 16 the blade is brought up over his right shoulder; pose 17 the blade slides into the sheath on his back. RETURN, poses 18 to 20: pose 18 the hand rests on the hilt, the weight settling; pose 19 both hands come down toward guard; pose 20 he is almost in the first pose of his fighting idle (wide stance, knees bent, both fists in guard at waist height, katana sheathed). THE KATANA (important): whenever it is drawn it is ONE straight blade with a dark guard and a wrapped handle, the same length in every pose (about half of his body height), always complete, never shrinking, never doubled, never broken; the blade stays a solid shape with hard pixel edges; the fire is drawn as SOLID opaque pixel clusters (deep red, orange, amber, pale yellow) hugging the blade, never transparent, never blurred, never a glow halo, never covering the blade shape; when sheathed it is whole on his back. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally, his body stays at about the same place, the feet are on the same ground line in all cells (about 8% from the bottom of the cell), and his whole body from the top of his hair to his boots fills about 55% of the cell height, leaving room above him for the raised blade and the flame; the whole blade and flame stay inside the cell with a safe margin; same scale in all 20 cells. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose (a calm focused face). No dust, no impact marks.""",
  jutsu_20="""Row 1: FIRE JUTSU (seals, then releasing a dragon), 20 poses, read in order (each row left to right: 5 poses per row, row 1 = poses 1 to 5, row 2 = poses 6 to 10, row 3 = poses 11 to 15, row 4 = poses 16 to 20). Kaen keeps the katana sheathed, performs hand seals, inhales deeply and thrusts both hands forward to release a fire dragon (the dragon itself is NOT drawn, it is added later as a separate effect). NATURALNESS (mandatory rule): movement never appears out of nowhere, it HAPPENS; every pose grows out of the previous one. Pose 1 is EXACTLY the landing pose of his hop (knees bent, torso leaning forward, left hand forward in guard, the right hand on the katana hilt behind his shoulder). SETTLE, poses 1 to 3: the right hand lets go of the hilt, the stance widens and steadies, both hands come together in front of his chest; pose 3 the palms are pressed together. SEALS, poses 4 to 10: a clear sequence of hand seals, one distinct hand shape every pose or two: pose 4 palms together, fingers up; pose 5 the right fist is covered by the left hand; pose 6 the fingers interlock, the two index fingers pointing up; pose 7 both hands flip over, the thumbs crossed; pose 8 the hands separate and cross in an X in front of his chest; pose 9 the hands come together again in a final seal at chest height, the eyes closed, the body tensed; pose 10 a short pause, the knees bending more, the weight loaded. CHARGE, poses 11 to 14: pose 11 he inhales, the chest swells, the shoulders rise, the body leans slightly back; pose 12 the chest is at its biggest, the head tilted back a little, the hands held at his chest; pose 13 the scarf and the hair lift as if pulled by a hot wind; pose 14 the body is fully loaded, the head comes forward again, the eyes open sharp and focused. RELEASE, poses 15 to 17: pose 15 he lunges one step forward and both arms start to thrust forward with the palms open; pose 16 the arms are fully extended forward with the palms open (the strongest pose of the whole jutsu, the front knee bent deep, the back leg straight, the scarf blown backward); pose 17 he holds the pose, the arms still extended, the body slightly shaking from the force. RECOVER, poses 18 to 20: pose 18 he exhales, the arms lower slowly, the shoulders drop; pose 19 he straightens, the right hand goes back toward the hilt behind his shoulder; pose 20 he is almost in the first pose of his fighting idle (wide stance, knees bent, both fists in guard at waist height, katana sheathed). THE KATANA (important): it stays sheathed on his back in every pose, whole, never drawn, never shrinking, never doubled. EFFECTS (important): NO dragon, NO flames, NO fire, NO smoke, NO glow, NO energy, NO motion blur and NO trails anywhere in any cell; the cells contain only the character, clean, on the flat green background. HANDS (important): in every pose each hand is a clean, separate shape with visible fingers, never fused with the other hand, the body or the scarf. FRAMING (important): in every cell the character is centered horizontally, his body stays at about the same place, the feet are on the same ground line in all cells (about 8% from the bottom of the cell), and his whole body from the top of his hair to his boots fills about 65% of the cell height; the whole body stays inside the cell with a safe margin; same scale in all 20 cells. IDENTITY (important): his hair spikes (same shape and count), face and expression are IDENTICAL in every pose (a calm focused face; only the eyes may close in poses 9 to 10, and open again in pose 14).""",
